  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1225 PM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nSomewhat of an active morning generally along and north of the I-\n40 corridor with thunderstorms ongoing. Much of the thunderstorm\nactivity has struggled with CIN still present after the overnight\nhours and the outflow boundaries from the storms continue to move\naway from the complex itself. Some additional thunderstorms are\nbeginning to fire across the Delta region, which will be\nsomething to watch through the remainder of the day. With this\noutflow boundary, this could provide enough lift to initialize\nadditional thunderstorms into the afternoon and evening hours,\nparticularly south of the I-40 corridor and especially into north\nMS. Around this same timeframe, an additional complex of storms\nis expected to develop to our west, providing additional lift\nacross the Mid-South. There are still several variables at play,\nincluding generally weak flow aloft and little to no shear\navailable. There will generally be > 2500 J/kg of SBCAPE with\nDCAPE values around 1000 J/kg, so some instances of damaging wind\ngusts and sub-severe hail cannot be ruled out. The main timing\nfor this threat to materialize would generally be from 3 PM to\n11 PM. Much of the current CAM guidance has this activity slowly\nwaning following sunset, with only a few lingering showers and\nthunderstorms into the overnight hours. With generally low dew\npoint depressions and any rainfall from earlier in the day, do\nexpect some patchy fog to develop across the area overnight\nthrough around sunrise Thursday morning.\n\nInto Thursday, the forecast will become largely dependent on how\nsouth the aforementioned complex of storms progresses overnight.\nWhile the majority of the Mid-South will likely remain dry, some\nshowers and thunderstorms cannot necessarily be ruled out along\nand south of this boundary that develops. The general thinking at\nthis point is that the boundary will be far enough south to keep\nmuch of any activity out of the Mid-South, but will leave some\nisolated to scattered PoPs across our southern tier of counties\ninto the afternoon hours on Thursday. Otherwise, clearing\nareawide is expected into Friday with high temperatures spanning\nthe upper 80s to low 90s.\n\nBy Saturday, an upper-level ridge will continue to move across\nthe Plains, shifting over the area into the beginning of next\nweek. This will allow a much drier weather pattern to emerge with\ntemperatures likely returning to above-normal through much of\nnext week. Dew point temperatures will remain somewhat moderated\nover this same period, so heat headlines may not necessarily be\nneeded despite the mid to upper 90 high temperatures. Nearing the\nend of the current forecast period, there really is no indication\nof breaking out of this pattern. For this recent, expect dry and\nhot conditions to persist through at least the middle of next\nweek.",
